The '''2002 Hawaii gubernatorial election''' was held on November 5, 2002, to select the [[governor of Hawaii]]. Incumbent [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democratic]] [[Governor of Hawaii]] [[Ben Cayetano]] was term-limited and therefore could not run for re-election. Former [[Mayor of Maui|Maui Mayor]] [[Linda Lingle]], who had narrowly lost the [[1998 Hawaii gubernatorial election|1998 election]], was nominated once again by the [[Republican Party (United States)|Republicans]] while [[Lieutenant Governor of Hawaii|Lieutenant Governor]] [[Mazie Hirono]] earned the [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democratic]] nomination in a tight race. Lingle and Hirono duked it out in a hard-fought campaign, with Hirono's campaign crippled by allegations of corruption within the [[Hawaii Democratic Party]] and many voters desiring a change.<ref name="nyt2002">{{cite news |last1=Broder |first1=John M. |title=The 2002 Campaign: The Democrats – Hawaii Democrats Reeling After Scandals and a Death |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2002/10/19/us/2002-campaign-democrats-hawaii-democrats-reeling-after-scandals-death.html |access-date=21 August 2019 |agency=The New York Times |date=19 October 2002}}</ref>

Ultimately Lingle defeated Hirono in a close election, making her the first [[Republican Party (United States)|Republican]] governor of Hawaii elected since [[1959 Hawaii gubernatorial election|1959]] and the state's first-ever female governor. She was the first white governor of the state since 1970. Lingle and Hirono faced off again in Hawaii’s [[2012 United States Senate election in Hawaii|2012 U.S. Senate election]], where Hirono won 63% - 37%, making Lingle the first female governor and Hirono the first female U.S. senator in Hawaii history.
