The '''2002 Maine gubernatorial election''' took place on November 5, 2002. [[Incumbent]] [[Independent politician|Independent]] [[Governor of Maine|Governor]] [[Angus King]] was term limited, and unable to seek re-election. [[U.S. Congressman]] [[John Baldacci]] won the [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democratic]] primary uncontested, while former [[Maine House of Representatives|State Representative]] [[Peter Cianchette]] emerged from the [[Republican Party (United States)|Republican]] primary victorious. Baldacci and Cianchetti squared off in the general election, along with [[Green Party (United States)|Green Party]] nominee [[Jonathan Carter (politician)|Jonathan Carter]] and [[Independent politician|independent]] [[Maine House of Representatives|State Representative]] [[John Michael (politician)|John Michael]].

Ultimately, Baldacci prevailed to win what would be his first of two terms as governor. John Baldacci's win marked the first Democratic gubernatorial victory in the state since [[1982 Maine gubernatorial election|1982]].<ref>{{Cite web |date=2011-04-25 |title=Baldacci, John E. {{!}} Maine: An Encyclopedia |url=https://maineanencyclopedia.com/john-e-baldacci/ |access-date=2023-01-12 |language=en-US}}</ref>
