The '''2002 Michigan gubernatorial election''' was one of the 36 United States [[Governor#United States|gubernatorial]] elections held on November 5, 2002. Incumbent [[Republican Party (United States)|Republican]] governor [[John Engler]], after serving three terms, was term-limited and was ineligible to run for a fourth term; his [[Lieutenant Governor of Michigan|lieutenant governor]] [[Dick Posthumus]], also a Republican, ran in his place. [[Jennifer Granholm]], then [[Attorney General of Michigan]], ran on the [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democratic Party]] ticket. Douglas Campbell ran on the [[Green Party (United States)|Green Party]] ticket, and Joseph M. Pilchak<ref name="Pilchak">The Political Graveyard: Index to Politicians http://politicalgraveyard.com/bio/pikiel-pinchot.html</ref> ran on the [[Constitution Party (United States)|Constitution Party]]<ref>The Constitution Party is still on the Michigan ballot as the United States Taxpayers' Party in Michigan.  Although the party changed its name in 1999, the Michigan Bureau of Elections does not provide any mechanism for a political party changing its name.</ref> ticket.

Granholm won with 51% of the vote, followed by Posthumus' 47%, Campbell with 1%, and Pilchak with less than 1%.<ref>[http://www.cnn.com/ELECTION/2002/pages/governor/ CNN.com Election 2002 – Governor]. [[CNN]].</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=http://miboecfr.nicusa.com/election/results/02GEN/02000000.html|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20140113134507/http://miboecfr.nicusa.com/election/results/02GEN/02000000.html|url-status=dead|title=2002 Official Michigan General Election Results – Governor 4 Year Term (1) Position|archivedate=January 13, 2014}}</ref>  This made Granholm the first female Michigan governor and the first Democratic governor of Michigan in 12 years.<ref>{{cite news 
