The '''2002 New York gubernatorial election''' was held on November 5, 2002. [[Republican Party (United States)|Republican]] governor [[George Pataki]] was re-elected to a third term, defeating [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democrat]] [[Carl McCall]] and [[Rochester, New York|Rochester]] billionaire [[Tom Golisano]], who ran on the [[Independence Party of New York|Independence Party]] line. {{As of|2023}}, this was the last time a Republican won a statewide election in New York, and the last time [[Albany County, New York|Albany]], [[Tompkins County, New York|Tompkins]] and [[Westchester County, New York|Westchester]] counties have voted Republican in a statewide election.

On Election Day, Pataki was easily re-elected, but fell short of receiving 50% of the vote. McCall received 33% of the vote, carrying New York City (other than Staten Island) and nearly carrying Albany County. In contrast to the norm for multiple third party campaigns, Golisano did better than his previous elections, receiving 14% of the vote and carrying his home county of [[Monroe County, New York|Monroe]] in [[western New York]].
