The '''2002 Pennsylvania gubernatorial election''' was held on November 5, 2002, to elect the [[Governor of Pennsylvania|governor]] and [[Lieutenant Governor of Pennsylvania]]. Incumbent [[Republican Party (United States)|Republican]] governor [[Mark Schweiker]], who took office in 2001 when [[Tom Ridge]] resigned to become [[Homeland Security Advisor]], was eligible to run for a full term, but did not do so. [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democrat]] [[Ed Rendell]], the former [[mayor of Philadelphia]] and [[Chairman of the Democratic National Committee]], emerged from a competitive primary to win the general election against Republican [[Pennsylvania Attorney General]] [[D. Michael Fisher|Mike Fisher]].

Rendell won the election, with commentators attributing his victory to "endless retail politicking" and a hard-working campaign.<ref name=general02 /> The political website ''[[PoliticsPA]]'' praised Rendell's campaign team of [[David L. Cohen]], [[David W. Sweet]], and [[Sandi Vito]].<ref name=general02>{{Cite web | title = The Best and Worst Campaigns'02| work = [[PoliticsPA]]| publisher = The Publius Group| date = 2002| url = http://www.politicspa.com/FEATURES/best+and+worst2.htm}}{{dead link|date=September 2016|bot=medic}}{{cbignore|bot=medic}}</ref> Fisher's strategy backfired; Rendell performed well in much of Eastern Pennsylvania and he was able to win by huge margins in even many traditionally Republican suburbs.<ref name=kennedy>{{cite book|last=Kennedy|first=John J.|title=Pennsylvania Elections: Statewide Contests From 1950–2004|year=2006|publisher=University Press of America|isbn=9780761832799|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=1J_9q-lIWRkC&q=1998+ridge+itkin}}</ref>

Rendell was the first official from Philadelphia to win the governorship since 1914.<ref name=kennedy/> This was the first time since 1826 that a Democrat won all four of Philadelphia's suburban counties and the first time since 1970 that a Democrat won [[Montgomery County, Pennsylvania|Montgomery County]] in a gubernatorial election. 
