The '''2002 Texas gubernatorial election''' was held on November 5, 2002, to elect the [[governor of Texas]]. Incumbent [[Republican Party (United States)|Republican]] governor [[Rick Perry]], who had ascended to the governorship after the resignation of [[George W. Bush]] to become President of the United States, was elected to his first full term in office, winning 58% of the vote to [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democrat]] [[Tony Sanchez (politician)|Tony Sanchez]]'s 40%.

Perry carried 218 out of 254 counties, while Sanchez only carried 36. Exit polls showed Perry easily won among white voters with 72% while Sanchez won the African American vote with 85% and the Latino vote with 65%. His second inauguration for a first full four-year term began on January 21, 2003, on the Texas State Capitol South Grounds.

{{As of|2022}}, this was the last time the Republican candidate carried [[Dallas County, Texas|Dallas County]], and the last time Republicans kept the vote margins within the single digits in [[Travis County, Texas|Travis County]].
